data: {"type":"data-source","data":{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configure-o3/overview.mdx","name":"overview.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}}

data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-e7b575b4-ac46-4939-8f56-849c863b606a","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/configure-o3/overview.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n130: - Changes to global properties packaged into the backend WAR require rebuilding the backend WAR\n131: - Changes to global properties in the application data directory take effect on the next backend restart (no rebuild needed)\n132: - For a comprehensive list of available global properties, see the [OpenMRS Global Properties documentation](https://wiki.openmrs.org/display/docs/Global+Properties) or check the documentation for specific modules\n133: \n134: <Callout emoji=\"💡\" type=\"info\">\n135:   **Concept dictionary management**: The recommended approach for managing concept dictionaries in OpenMRS is to set up your own organization and resources in [Open Concept Lab (OCL)](https://app.openconceptlab.org/). This allows you to standardize concept management across your implementation, create and maintain your own concept sets, and export them as ZIP files for inclusion in content packages. The [CIEL dictionary](https://app.openconceptlab.org/#/orgs/CIEL/sources/CIEL/) is the standard concept dictionary containing thousands of clinical concepts—you can reference CIEL concepts in your OCL resources or create organization-specific concepts. When configuring drugs, forms, or other metadata, you can search OCL to find concept UUIDs and manage your concept sets through your OCL organization.\n136: </Callout>\n137: \n138: **File organization**: Configuration files are organized by domain in subdirectories (e.g., `configuration/concepts/`, `configuration/locations/`, `configuration/drugs/`, `configuration/ampathforms/`). Each domain has specific file format requirements and naming conventions documented in the Initializer README. Files can be organized in nested directory structures, and Initializer will process them in alphabetical order if no explicit order is specified.\n139: \n140: **Practical examples**:\n141: - **Locations CSV** - Each row defines a location with UUID, name, description, parent location, and flags (e.g., whether it's a login location)\n142: - **Drugs CSV** - Each row creates a drug entry with UUID, name, concept UUID (from CIEL/OCL), form concept, and strength (e.g., \"Paracetamol 500mg tablet\")\n143: - **Forms JSON** - Forms built in Form Builder can be exported as JSON and placed in the forms configuration directory\n144: - **Form translations** - Separate translation files map form label texts to translated strings for different languages\n145: \n146: **Runtime control**: The Initializer module provides runtime properties that allow you to:\n147: - Enable or disable specific domains during initialization\n148: - Control loading behavior (e.g., fail-fast vs. continue on errors)\n149: - Configure logging verbosity\n150: - Filter files using wildcard patterns\n151: \n152: For detailed information on file formats, naming conventions, domain-specific requirements, and runtime properties, see the [Initializer module's README](https://github.com/mekomsolutions/openmrs-module-initializer#openmrs-initializer-module). The README includes specific sub-documentation for each config file type (e.g., drugs CSV, locations, form translations).\n153: \n154: **Getting help**: If you have questions or encounter issues with Initializer configuration, the OpenMRS community recommends posting on the [OpenMRS Talk forum](https://talk.openmrs.org/) and tagging your topic with \"initializer\" so community members can help.\n155: \n156: ## Configuring individual frontend modules\n157: \n158: Per-app configuration is available in O3 via the built-in [configuration system](/docs/configuration-system). Each frontend module can define its own configuration schema in a `config-schema.ts` file inside its `src` directory. The following snippet is drawn from the Programs frontend module's config-schema:\n159: \n160: ```tsx filename=\"src/config-schema.ts\"\n161: import { Type } from '@openmrs/esm-framework';\n162: \n163: export const configSchema = {\n164:   hideAddProgramButton: {\n165:     _type: Type.Boolean,\n166:     _default: false,\n167:   },\n168:   showProgramStatusField: {\n169:     _type: Type.Boolean,\n170:     _description:\n171:       'Whether to show the Program status field in the Record program enrollment and Edit program enrollment forms. If set to true, the `Program status` field is displayed in the Programs datatable',\n172:     _default: false,\n173:   },\n174: };\n175: \n176: export interface ConfigObject {\n177:   hideAddProgramButton: boolean;\n178:   showProgramStatusField: boolean;\n179: }\n180: ```\n181: \n182: The properties specified in the config schema allow implementers to tailor the behavior of the module to their needs. The configuration system also allows implementers to specify default values for the various configuration properties. \n183: \n\n(Output capped at 5KB. Showing lines 130-183 of 391. Use offset=184 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configure-o3/overview.mdx","name":"overview.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

data: {"type":"data-source","data":{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}}

data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-0ec4280b-ac77-4395-a012-77fb8acafcee","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n220:   const config = await getConfig(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\");\n221:   return \"Hello, my name is Dr. \" + config.virtualProvider.name.given.join(\" \");\n222: }\n223: ```\n224: \n225: The content of config will be pulled from the config files, falling back to the defaults for configuration elements for which no values have been provided.\n226: \n227: **How configuration values are merged:**\n228: \n229: When multiple configuration sources provide values for the same key, they are merged with the following precedence (later sources override earlier ones):\n230: \n231: 1. **Defaults** from the schema definition\n232: 2. **Configuration files** (merged in the order they are loaded)\n233: 3. **Temporary config** from the Implementer Tools UI (highest precedence)\n234: \n235: This means that if you set a value in a config file and then change it using the Implementer Tools, the Implementer Tools value will take effect. Similarly, if the same key appears in multiple config files, the value from the last file loaded will be used.\n236: \n237: Nested objects are merged deeply, but arrays are replaced as whole values. If a later config source provides an array for a key, it replaces the earlier array rather than merging by index.\n238: \n239: #### React support\n240: \n241: A React Hook is provided to hide the asynchronicity of config loading. The `useConfig` hook automatically determines which module's configuration to load based on the `ComponentContext`, which is set when you use `getSyncLifecycle` or `getAsyncLifecycle` with a `moduleName`.\n242: \n243: ```ts\n244: import HologramDoctor from \"./hologram-doctor.component\";\n245: \n246: export const hologramDoctor = getSyncLifecycle(HologramDoctor, {\n247:   featureName: 'hologram doctor',\n248:   moduleName: '@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor',\n249: });\n250: ```\n251: \n252: You can then get the config tree as an object using the `useConfig` React hook:\n253: \n254: ```ts\n255: import { useConfig } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n256: \n257: export default function DoctorGreeting() {\n258:   const config = useConfig();\n259:   const greeting = \"Hello, my name is Dr. \" + config.virtualProvider.name.given.join(\" \");\n260:   return <div>{greeting}</div>;\n261: }\n262: ```\n263: \n264: The `useConfig` hook automatically:\n265: - Loads the module's configuration based on the `moduleName` from the `ComponentContext`\n266: - Merges extension-specific configuration when used within an extension component\n267: - Falls back to defaults for configuration elements for which no values have been provided\n268: \n269: **Loading configuration from another module:**\n270: \n271: You can optionally load configuration from a different module using the `externalModuleName` option. This should only be used when absolutely necessary, as it can create coupling between modules:\n272: \n273: ```ts\n274: const otherModuleConfig = useConfig({ externalModuleName: \"@openmrs/esm-other-module\" });\n275: ```\n276: \n277: **Registering config schemas:**\n278: \n279: In Core v5+ modules, config schemas should be registered in the `startupApp()` function:\n280: \n281: ```ts\n282: import { defineConfigSchema } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n283: import { configSchema } from \"./config-schema\";\n284: \n285: const moduleName = \"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\";\n286: \n287: export function startupApp() {\n288:   defineConfigSchema(moduleName, configSchema);\n289: }\n290: ```\n291: \n292: This ensures the schema is registered before the module's components are rendered.\n293: \n294: **Automatic schema features:**\n295: \n296: Every module automatically receives an implicit schema that includes two reserved configuration keys:\n297: \n298: - **`Display conditions`** - Used by the extension system to control extension visibility (only relevant for extensions)\n299: - **`Translation overrides`** - Used for per-language translation overrides. See [Configure translations](/docs/configure-o3/configure-translations) for details on how implementers use this feature.\n300: \n301: This implicit schema is available immediately, even before you call `defineConfigSchema()`. This allows translation overrides to be loaded early, which is important because the translation system requires them before modules can fully initialize. When you define your custom schema, it is automatically merged with the implicit schema, so you don't need to (and should not) define these reserved keys yourself.\n302: \n303: #### Support in other frameworks (Angular, Vue, Svelte, etc.)\n304: \n305: This hasn't been implemented yet, but you are welcome to implement it! See the [Contributing guide](/docs/frontend-modules/contributing).\n306: \n307: #### Typing\n308: \n309: It is nice to be able to have type validation for your use of configuration. To accomplish this, define an interface alongside your config schema.\n310: \n311: ```ts\n312: import { defineConfigSchema, Type } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n313: \n314: defineConfigSchema(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\", {\n315:   hologram: {\n316:     color: {\n317:       _type: Type.Boolean,\n318:       _default: false,\n319:       _description: \"Whether the hologram supports color display.\",\n320:     },\n321:   },\n322: });\n323: \n324: export interface HologramDoctorConfig {\n325:   hologram: {\n326:     color: boolean;\n327:   };\n328: }\n329: ```\n330: \n331: You can then use this typing information when calling the config functions:\n332: \n333: ```ts\n334: const config = useConfig<HologramDoctorConfig>();\n335: ```\n336: \n337: #### Extension-specific configuration\n338: \n339: Extensions can have their own configuration schemas that are separate from their parent module's configuration. This is useful when an extension needs configuration options that don't apply to the module as a whole.\n340: \n\n(Output capped at 5KB. Showing lines 220-340 of 439. Use offset=341 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

