data: {"type":"data-source","data":{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}}

data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-85c2192b-8e75-4033-a8e5-ea6832a513e7","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n1: import { Callout } from \"nextra/components\";\n2: \n3: # The Configuration System\n4: \n5: Introduced by [RFC-14](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-rfc-frontend/blob/master/text/0014-configuration.md), the configuration system provides a framework for making frontend modules configurable by implementers without requiring code changes. It enables developers to define schemas that specify what configuration options are available, their types, defaults, and validation rules.\n6: \n7: <Callout emoji=\"ℹ️\" type=\"info\">\n8:   **For implementers**: If you're looking to configure an existing O3 instance (changing logos, translations, module settings, etc.), see the [Configure O3](/docs/configure-o3/overview) guide instead. This guide is for **developers** who want to make their modules configurable.\n9: </Callout>\n10: \n11: ## Using the Configuration System\n12: \n13: A framework is provided to make configurability easier for developers and configuring easier for implementers.\n14: \n15: **For implementers**: To learn how to configure your O3 instance, see the [Configure O3](/docs/configure-o3/overview) guide, which covers practical configuration tasks like branding, translations, and module-specific settings.\n16: \n17: ### How to make a frontend module configurable\n18: \n19: You should use the OpenMRS Frontend Framework to make modules configurable.\n20: \n21: The main task is to create a config schema for your module. The config schema is what tells the framework what configuration files should look like, including defaults and validations.\n22: \n23: **Note**: Configuration schemas are optional. If your module doesn't need any configuration options (for example, if it's a library module like the [React Form Engine](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-form-engine-lib) that receives all its configuration through props or function parameters), you don't need to define a schema. The configuration system will still work; modules without schemas use the implicit schema for `Display conditions` and `Translation overrides`.\n24: \n25: #### Designing a schema\n26: \n27: You’ll probably start with some idea of what you want configs for your module to look like. Try and put yourself in the implementer’s shoes and imagine what features they will expect to be configurable, and what they might expect the configuration property to be called. Assume they don’t know anything about the internal workings of your module.\n28: \n29: By way of example, let’s say we’re building a module for a virtual provider functionality at a very futuristic hospital. Maybe we want an implementer to be able to write the following in their config file:\n30: \n31: ```ts\n32: \"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\": {\n33:   \"hologram\": {\n34:     \"color\": true\n35:   },\n36:   \"virtualProvider\": {\n37:     \"name\": {\n38:       \"given\": [\"Qui\", \"Gon\"]\n39:     }\n40:   },\n41:   \"robots\": [\n42:     { \"name\": \"R2-D2\", \"homeworld\": \"Naboo\" },\n43:     { \"name\": \"BB-8\", \"homeworld\": \"Hosnian Prime\" }\n44:   ]\n45: }\n46: ```\n47: \n48: In the following section, we’ll see how to write a config schema that supports these config elements.\n49: \n50: #### Defining a schema\n51: \n52: We’ll start with just that first nested config element from above, `hologram.color`. We must provide defaults for all of the values—in OpenMRS Frontend 3.0, all configuration is optional. All modules should do something reasonable out of the box.\n53: \n54: ```ts\n55: import { defineConfigSchema, Type } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n56: \n57: defineConfigSchema(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\", {\n58:   hologram: {\n59:     color: {\n60:       _type: Type.Boolean,\n61:       _default: false,\n62:       _description: \"Whether the hologram supports color display.\"\n63:     }\n64:   }\n65: });\n66: ```\n67: \n68: Note that each configuration element should have an object for a value, and that this object must define the properties for that element. Do not do this:\n69: \n70: ```ts\n71: // This is wrong. `salutation` is a raw value, not a schema object.\n72: defineConfigSchema(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\", {\n73:   hologram: {\n74:     salutation: \"Some friendly default salutation!\"\n75:   }\n76: });\n77: ```\n78: \n79: The words prefixed with `_` are schema keywords. Do not prefix the names of your config elements with underscores. Especially do not use a schema keyword as a config element name.\n80: \n81: **Reserved configuration keys:**\n82: \n83: The following keys are reserved and cannot be used as configuration element names:\n84: - `Display conditions` - Used by the extension system for extension visibility control\n85: - `Translation overrides` - Used for per-language translation overrides\n86: \n87: These keys are automatically included in every module's implicit schema and will cause validation errors if you try to use them as custom configuration keys.\n88: \n89: ```ts\n90: // Don't do this. `_default` is a schema keyword, not a config element name.\n91: defineConfigSchema(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\", {\n92:   hologram: {\n93:     salutation: {\n94:       _default: {\n95:         _default: \"Greetings\"\n96:       }\n97:     }\n98:   }\n99: });\n100: ```\n101: \n102: #### Typing\n103: \n104: While not strictly required in the current version, you should provide a type for every config element you define. The `_type` keyword accepts values from the Type enum.\n105: \n106: The available types are:\n107: - `Type.String` - String values\n108: - `Type.Number` - Numeric values\n109: - `Type.Boolean` - Boolean values (true/false)\n\n(Output capped at 5KB. Showing lines 1-109 of 439. Use offset=110 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

