data: {"type":"data-source","data":{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}}

data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-06ae12c9-8814-4264-aeb3-61f523f9b8a1","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n109: - `Type.Boolean` - Boolean values (true/false)\n110: - `Type.Array` - Arrays (requires `_elements` to specify the array element schema)\n111: - `Type.Object` - Freeform object values. When used with `_elements`, O3 validates and fills defaults for each value in the object.\n112: - `Type.UUID` - UUID strings\n113: - `Type.ConceptUuid` - Concept UUID strings\n114: - `Type.PersonAttributeTypeUuid` - Person attribute type UUID strings\n115: - `Type.PatientIdentifierTypeUuid` - Patient identifier type UUID strings\n116: \n117: These types are used both to validate input and to support special behavior in the implementer tools.\n118: \n119: #### Validators\n120: \n121: You should provide validators for your configuration elements wherever possible. This reduces the probability that implementers using your module will have hard-to-debug runtime errors. It gives you, the module developer, the opportunity to provide implementers with very helpful explanations about why their configuration doesn’t work.\n122: \n123: ```ts\n124: robot: {\n125:   name: {\n126:     _type: Type.String,\n127:     _default: \"R2D2\",\n128:     _description: \"What to call the robot\",\n129:     _validators: [\n130:       validator(n => /\\d/.test(n), \"Robots must have numbers in their names\")\n131:     ]\n132:   }\n133: }\n134: ```\n135: \n136: (Note that this piece of schema is not part of our above example. It only supports a single robot, whereas we need to allow the implementer to provide an array of robots).\n137: \n138: A validator can be created using the `validator` function, as above.\n139: \n140: The first argument is a function that takes the config value as its only argument. If the function returns `true`, validation passes. If it returns `false`, validation fails and the second argument to `validator` is used as the error message. You can also provide a custom validator function directly in `_validators`; direct validators should return `undefined` for success or an error string for failure.\n141: \n142: **Important**: When validation fails, the error is logged to the browser console, but the application continues to run. Invalid configuration values are still used—validation errors serve as warnings to help implementers identify and fix configuration problems, rather than preventing the application from starting.\n143: \n144: You can even validate nested objects:\n145: \n146: ```ts\n147: colorPicker: {\n148:   options: { _default: [\"green\", \"red\", \"blue\"] },\n149:   initial: { _default: \"green\" },\n150:   _description: \"The color picker for lightsabers\",\n151:   _validators: [\n152:     validator(o => o.options.includes(o.initial),\n153:       \"Initial must be one of the options\")\n154:   ]\n155: }\n156: ```\n157: \n158: For convenience, some common validators are provided out of the box, including [`inRange`](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-core/blob/main/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/inRange.md), [`oneOf`](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-core/blob/main/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/oneOf.md), and [`isUrlWithTemplateParameters`](https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-esm-core/blob/main/packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/isUrlWithTemplateParameters.md).\n159: \n160: #### Arrays\n161: \n162: You can accept and validate arrays, and arrays containing objects, in your configuration schema. This is configured with the `_elements` parameter, used with `_type: Type.Array`. For example, a schema which would accept an array of strings up to 30 characters long would look like this:\n163: \n164: ```ts\n165: virtualProvider: {\n166:   name: {\n167:     given: {\n168:       _type: Type.Array,\n169:       _default: [\"Obi\", \"Wan\"],\n170:       _elements: {\n171:         _type: Type.String,\n172:         _validators: [validator(n => n.length < 30, \"Must be less than 30 characters\")]\n173:       }\n174:     },\n175:     _description: \"The name of the avatar. Does not have to be the name of the actual provider\"\n176:   },\n177:   _description: \"The avatar of the medical practitioner\"\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: Here is an example of a schema that expects an array of objects structured in a particular way.\n182: \n183: ```ts\n184: robots: {\n185:   _type: Type.Array,\n186:   _default: [\n187:     { name: \"R2-D2\", homeworld: \"Naboo\" },\n188:     { name: \"C-3PO\", homeworld: \"Tatooine\" }\n189:   ],\n190:   _description: \"The list of all available robots\",\n191:   _elements: {\n192:     name: {\n193:       _type: Type.String,\n194:       _description: \"What to call the robot\",\n195:       _validators: [robotNameValidator]\n196:     },\n197:     homeworld: {\n198:       _type: Type.String,\n199:       _description: \"Where the robot is from\",\n200:       _default: null  // not required\n201:     }\n202:   }\n203: }\n204: ```\n205: \n206: This schema validates the keys and value types of objects in the `robots` array. Unknown keys are logged as configuration errors, and defaults are filled in when an `_elements` entry provides an `_default`.\n207: \n208: Objects within arrays do not have to provide defaults for every key. If a field such as `name` is required for your module to work correctly, add a validator that checks each object and returns a helpful error message when the field is missing.\n209: \n210: #### Using config values\n211: \n212: ##### The generic way\n213: \n214: The config is fetched asynchronously using `getConfig(moduleName)`. Continuing the above example, we would have something like:\n215: \n216: ```ts\n217: import { getConfig } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n218: \n219: async function doctorGreeting() {\n220:   const config = await getConfig(\"@openmrs/esm-hologram-doctor\");\n\n(Showing lines 109-220 of 439. Use offset=221 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/configuration-system.mdx","name":"configuration-system.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

